---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-and-seasonal-affective-disorder`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D and Seasonal Affective Disorder',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  about: [{ '@type': 'MedicalCondition', name: 'Seasonal affective disorder', code: { '@type': 'MedicalCode', codeValue: 'F33', codingSystem: 'ICD-10' } }],
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D and SAD', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D and Seasonal Affective Disorder (SAD): Two Different Winter Problems"
  description="SAD lamps vs vitamin D — they treat different things. SAD is a circadian problem; vitamin D deficiency is an endocrine one. Both can coexist in winter."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-and-seasonal-affective-disorder", label: "Vitamin D & SAD" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D and Seasonal Affective Disorder</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Both vitamin D deficiency and seasonal affective disorder (SAD) intensify in
      winter and can produce overlapping symptoms — low mood, fatigue, sleep changes.
      They are, however, distinct problems with different mechanisms and different
      treatments. Both can be present at once; getting the right diagnosis matters.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">SAD — a circadian and phototransduction problem</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      SAD is a recurrent major depressive disorder with seasonal pattern (usually
      autumn-winter onset, spring remission). Onset correlates with day length and
      light exposure; the core biology involves reduced retinal light input,
      dysregulated melatonin timing, and (in some patients) reduced serotonin
      turnover in winter. It responds to bright light therapy (10,000 lux for 30
      minutes in the morning), SSRIs, and CBT — not to vitamin D specifically.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Vitamin D deficiency — an endocrine problem</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Winter vitamin D deficiency has its own mood effects — chronic musculoskeletal
      pain, fatigue, lower mood scores in some studies. Meta-analyses suggest
      correcting deficiency modestly improves depression symptoms in patients with
      baseline &lt; 20 ng/mL 25(OH)D. It does not treat classical SAD; it treats
      the deficiency-associated mood component.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The overlap</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Winter northern-latitude adults with mood symptoms often have both problems.
      Days are short (SAD trigger) and 25(OH)D is low (deficiency). Ideal management
      addresses both: bright light box for SAD + vitamin D repletion for deficiency.
      Neither substitutes for the other.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Bright light box ≠ UVB lamp</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Standard 10,000-lux SAD lamps emit visible white or full-spectrum light with
      minimal UV. They do not produce vitamin D. Marketing that conflates the two is
      misleading. See our <a href="/uv-lamps-for-vitamin-d" class="text-amber-700 underline">UV lamps page</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Trial evidence</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Kjaergaard 2012</strong> — high-dose vitamin D vs placebo in overweight subjects with low mood; some symptom improvement.</li>
      <li><strong>Frandsen 2014</strong> — 2,800 IU/day in patients with SAD; no additional benefit over placebo when both had winter light exposure.</li>
      <li><strong>Cochrane 2020 review</strong> — vitamin D for depression: small overall effect, larger effect in deficient patients.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Practical winter mood plan</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>Get morning bright light exposure — outdoor walk within 1 hour of waking is ideal; 10,000-lux light box is a substitute.</li>
      <li>Correct vitamin D deficiency — 2,000 IU/day baseline; test if symptoms are significant.</li>
      <li>Regular exercise and sleep hygiene — dose-dependent effects on mood in both SAD and general depression.</li>
      <li>See a clinician for persistent symptoms — SSRIs (bupropion is uniquely FDA-approved for SAD prevention) and CBT have strong evidence.</li>
      <li>Do not use bright light therapy alone if you have bipolar disorder without psychiatric input — can trigger hypomania.</li>
    </ol>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Persistent depressed mood, especially with
      suicidal ideation, deserves urgent mental health evaluation — not a vitamin bottle.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-and-seasonal-affective-disorder" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
